\ `swap` relabels its two entries without moving them, so a quotation literal swapped under a value
\ can sit at an index whose physical slot still holds the value another entry reads. Building the
\ quotation must not overwrite that slot. Nothing here sets a compile flag, so the eager pass checks
\ the compiled run against the interpreted golden.

use "testing" ;

\ After the first swap, `b` lives in slot 1. The second swap leaves the quotation at index 1, and
\ `drop` exposes it as the word's output.
quotation-under-swap: ( a b -- b q ) [ swap [ 7 8 + ] swap drop ] ;

"a quotation swapped under a value" test-section
1 2 quotation-under-swap
call 15 "the quotation survives" assert=
2 "the value under it survives" assert=

\ The original report: a task handle swapped above a channel, then a quotation sent through it.
"a quotation sent to a spawned task" test-section
[
  <channel> dup
  [ receive call ] curry spawn
  swap [ 7 8 + ] swap send
  await 15 "await returns the task's result" assert=
] task-scope
